{
  "state": {
    "case_id": "182",
    "case_summary": "An adolescent girl presented with a soft tissue mass on her right flank that remained unchanged in size and appearance for several years. The patient was concerned about appearance and a feeling of fullness caused by the substantial convexity of the lesion. The condition had been present since infancy.\n\nExamination of the right flank revealed a skin-colored, exophytic, soft, oval tumor with overlying pinpoint vessels and branching telangiectasia. Initial measurements were 0.8 cm high and 7.5 cm wide.\n\n- Imaging Documentation:    Image Title: Pretreatment Clinical Image    Image Description: Baseline view of the lesion on the right flank measuring 0.8 cm high and 7.5 cm wide.    Image Title: Posttreatment Clinical Image    Image Description: The lesion shows substantial improvement 2 months after the second series of treatment; measurements are 0.2 cm high and 6.4 cm wide.  - Histological Findings:    The lesions are composed of mature adipose and fibrous tissues.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n\nOptions:\nA. Fibrolipoma\nB. Hamartoma\nC. Fibrofatty residua of involuted infantile hemangioma\nD. Lipoma\n",
